ern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-00bda344-6374-4736-b315-6c4dd7f47514"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Danske politikere må forsvare EU’s succesprogram for grundforskning","type":"Newspaper Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-797503","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background Childhood underweight, indicated by low body mass index (BMI) for age and sex, is associated with morbidity, mortality and poverty in low-income settings, but is often overlooked in high-income countries, due to unequivocal focus on overweight. This study examined the prevalence and socioeconomic characteristics of underweight among children and adolescents in Denmark during 2012–2019. Methods We used nationwide Danish register data on height and weight measured objectively in 4 61 041 6–7 year-olds and 3 65 312 14–15 year-olds during 2012–2019, covering 89.4% and 67.2% of all schoolchildren in these age groups. We calculated BMI z-scores and classified underweight using the International Obesity Task Force age- and sex-specific BMI cut-offs and linked with data on parental education and household income as indicators of socioeconomic circumstances. Results The prevalence of underweight remained relatively stable at~8.5% among 6–7-year-old girls during 2012–2019. Among 14–15-year-old girls, it was initially higher but decreased slightly (<1%-point) to 8.6% in 2019. Among boys, the prevalence increased from ~7% to just above 8% in both age groups during the period. Across all years, underweight was more common among 14–15 year-olds whose parents had long higher education (girls: 10.4%–12.1%; boys: 8.7%–10.2%) compared with primary school (girls: 5.6%–7.5%; boys: 6.1%–7.8%), with the most pronounced differences among girls. Similar differences across parental education were observed among 6–7 year-olds, although they were smaller. Less distinct but comparable differences in underweight prevalence were found across household income. Conclusions Underweight is prevalent but has remained relatively stable during the 2010s among Danish children and adolescents. Unlike the pattern reported in low-income settings, and that seen for overweight, underweight is associated with socioeconomic advantage in this high-income setting.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Aurup, Anne V.","Thygesen, Lau C","Klitgaard, Marie Borring","Thorsted, Anne","Strandberg-Larsen, Katrine","Damsgaard, Camilla Trab"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-819660"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"has underweight increased among children and adolescents during 000000002012 000000002019 and does the prevalence depend on socioeconomic circumstances a nationwide register based study from denmark","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjph-2024-002328","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-819660","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11934366","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"40134535","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-934f4eb2-b080-4cda-bfd4-4e9b5e5311a9"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-FCB7BF3145AD52824810BD4D9063FD74"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjph-2024-002328","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/455362859/e002328.full.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, Nutrition and Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["University of Southern Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Anne Vedelsdal","key":"auruppau","last":"Aurup","locid":"ccabd5a3-3fc1-4696-9bcd-feadbc4a5819","name":"Aurup, Anne V.","name_org":"Aurup, Anne Vedelsdal","orcid":"0000-0002-7309-5851","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Lau C","key":"thygesenpau","last":"Thygesen","name":"Thygesen, Lau C","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Marie Borring","key":"klitgaardpau","last":"Klitgaard","name":"Klitgaard, Marie Borring","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anne","key":"thorstedpau","last":"Thorsted","name":"Thorsted, Anne","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Katrine","key":"strandberglarsenpau","last":"Strandberg-Larsen","locid":"5a443538-3866-40a3-b93e-b4d4be24a83a","name":"Strandberg-Larsen, Katrine","orcid":"0000-0001-7061-3767","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Camilla Trab","key":"damsgaardpau","last":"Damsgaard","locid":"5ea9cc09-17f1-46a3-9e4e-fa5490abd815","name":"Damsgaard, Camilla Trab","orcid":"0000-0002-5013-0562","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40134535"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1136/bmjph-2024-002328","issn":["27534294"],"journal":"BMJ Public Health","paperid":"e002328","status":"p","title":"BMJ Public Health","volume":"3"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-934f4eb2-b080-4cda-bfd4-4e9b5e5311a9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Has underweight increased among children and adolescents during 2012–2019, and does the prevalence depend on socioeconomic circumstances? A nationwide register-based study from Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-819660","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Healthcare use among adolescents after vaccination against SARS-CoV-2 is unknown. In a real-life register-based cohort study (trial NCT04786353), healthcare use was compared among Pfizer-BioNTech BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccinated and unvaccinated 12–18-year-olds. First-dose-vaccinated (between 1 May and 30 September 2021) adolescents were sex and age matched 1:1 with unvaccinated adolescents. Outcomes were visits to emergency rooms, hospitalization, and visits to general practitioners and specialist practitioners. The prior event rate ratio (PERR) was applied. The study finds that boys had fewer visits to general practitioners (PERR 0.93, 95% confidence interval (CI) 0.89–0.99) after the first vaccine. Up to 56 days after the second dose, vaccinated boys had lower rates of visits to specialist practitioners (0.88, 95% CI 0.79–0.99); after 57–182 days, vaccinated girls and boys had higher rates of visits to emergency rooms (1.22, 95% CI 1.08–1.39; 1.17, 95% CI 1.07–1.31) and to general practitioners (1.17, 95% CI 1.12–1.21; 1.17, 95% CI 1.13–1.22). Furthermore, vaccinated boys had higher rates of visits to specialist practitioners (1.23, 95% CI 1.08–1.39). Estimates were close to one and do not indicate that BNT162b2 leads to a practically meaningful increase in healthcare use among vaccinated adolescents.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Berg, Selina Kikkenborg","Wallach-Kildemoes, Helle","Rasmussen, Line Ryberg","Nygaard, Ulrikka","Birk, Nina Marie","Bundgaard, Henning","Ersbøll, Annette Kjær","Thygesen, Lau Caspar","Nielsen, Susanne Dam","Christensen, Anne Vinggaard"],"cuid":["S02-093.62-814019"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Berg, Selina Kikkenborg"],"keyword":["Adolescent","BNT162 Vaccine","COVID 19 Vaccines administration dosage","COVID 19 prevention control","Child","Cohort Studies","Denmark epidemiology","Emergency Service Hospital statistics numerical data","Female","Hospitalization statistics numerical data","Humans","Male","Patient Acceptance of Health Care statistics numerical data","Registries","Vaccination statistics numerical data"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"healthcare use in 000000000012 000000000018 year old adolescents vaccinated against sars cov 000000000002 versus unvaccinated in a national register based danish cohort","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105003590644","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-093.62-814019","identifier":[{"id":"105003590644","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39833423","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-4ab42dd7-f95e-4919-9c67-81d729ba1a42","sdu-d12753a5-d954-481e-9d6d-a70a9166b0b7"],"keyword":["Adolescent","BNT162 Vaccine","COVID-19 Vaccines/administration & dosage","COVID-19/prevention & control","Child","Cohort Studies","Denmark/epidemiology","Emergency Service, Hospital/statistics & numerical data","Female","Hospitalization/statistics & numerical data","Humans","Male","Patient Acceptance of Health Care/statistics & numerical data","Registries","Unvaccinated Persons","Vaccination/statistics & numerical data"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-D1A9A086615EB828937F626E80713AF9"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Internal medicine: Cardiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health, Geographical and registerbased epidemiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Selina Kikkenborg","key":"bergpau","last":"Berg","locid":"92d15fb6-c29f-4d38-88a4-53d28ecc9a13","name":"Berg, Selina Kikkenborg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Helle","key":"wallachkildemoespau","last":"Wallach-Kildemoes","name":"Wallach-Kildemoes, Helle","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Line Ryberg","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Line Ryberg","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Ulrikka","key":"nygaardpau","last":"Nygaard","locid":"43b2e239-ddc1-4676-a8db-0a0f23906813","name":"Nygaard, Ulrikka","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nina Marie","key":"birkpau","last":"Birk","name":"Birk, Nina Marie","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Henning","key":"bundgaardpau","last":"Bundgaard","locid":"254c7f8d-79dd-4a5d-870f-3ed78cb56464","name":"Bundgaard, Henning","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Annette Kjær","key":"ersbøllpau","last":"Ersbøll","locid":"ba343517-a6c5-47c1-a8ba-4cbe00b7409c","name":"Ersbøll, Annette Kjær","orcid":"0000-0002-9407-3387","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Lau Caspar","key":"thygesenpau","last":"Thygesen","locid":"1932f287-552d-4539-8153-b2fc204a703b","name":"Thygesen, Lau Caspar","orcid":"0000-0001-8375-5211","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Susanne Dam","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"2baa36fa-2709-482a-9595-52c5b9537801","name":"Nielsen, Susanne Dam","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne Vinggaard","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Anne Vinggaard","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39833423"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41562-024-02097-y","issn":["23973374"],"issue":"4","journal":"Nature Human Behaviour","pages":"737-745","publisher":"Springer Nature","status":"p","title":"Nature Human Behaviour","title_org":"Nature Human Behavior","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105003590644","2-s2.0-85217204893"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4ab42dd7-f95e-4919-9c67-81d729ba1a42","sdu-d12753a5-d954-481e-9d6d-a70a9166b0b7"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Healthcare use in 12–18-year-old adolescents vaccinated against SARS-CoV-2 versus unvaccinated in a national register-based Danish cohort","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-093.62-814019","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: When golimumab (GLM) was approved for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis (UC) in 2013, a post-authorization safety study was conducted. OBJECTIVE: Our objective was to examine whether exposure to GLM was associated with an increased incidence of all-cause total colectomy, colorectal cancer, and hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma in Denmark and Sweden. METHODS: We conducted a new-user, active comparator cohort study of patients with UC in 2013-2021. Exposure to GLM, other anti-tumor necrosis factor (TNF) agents (infliximab and adalimumab) and thiopurines was a time-varying variable. Therapies were based on prescription redemptions and hospital-based administration of medications from national prescription and hospital registers. The association between exposure to study therapies and outcomes was evaluated using Poisson regression of incidence rates (IRs), presented as IR ratios (IRRs) and 95% confidence intervals (CIs). RESULTS: A total of 5177 and 7469 patients were included in Denmark and Sweden, respectively. The IR of all-cause total colectomy per 1000 person-years was higher in Denmark (IR 42.6; 95% CI 38.9-46.2) than in Sweden (IR 16.1; 95% CI 14.2-18.0). No significant difference was observed in all-cause total colectomy between GLM and other anti-TNF agents (Denmark: adjusted IRR [aIRR] 1.28; 95% CI 0.98-1.66; Sweden: aIRR 1.17; 95% CI 0.72-1.90). A significant difference was observed between GLM and thiopurines (Denmark: aIRR 13.62; 95% CI 8.73-21.26; Sweden: aIRR 4.52; 2.75-7.41). Privacy regulations prevented analysis of a few colorectal cancer events. No hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma events were identified. CONCLUSION: The IR of all-cause total colectomy with GLM was similar to that with other anti-TNF agents but was much higher than with thiopurines, probably related to confounding by indication.","author_count":20,"contributors":["Ersbøll, Annette Kjær","Huang, Zhiping","Hill, Deanna D.","Hede, Simone Møller","Andersen, Vibeke","Bolin, Kristian","Kristensen, Marie Skov","Esslinger, Suzan","Hansen, Frida Richter","Hertervig, Erik","Kallio, Lila","Kjærulff, Thora Majlund","Kloster, Stine","Krumme, Alexis","Lewis, James D.","Mehkri, Laila","Qvist, Niels","Thygesen, Lau Caspar","Weinstein, Cindy","Green, Anders"],"cuid":["S02-099.95-822996"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Ersbøll, Annette Kjær"],"country":["Finland","Sweden","Switzerland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Cilag GmbH International","Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania","Inc.","Janssen Research & Development","Johnson & Johnson","Merck & Co., Inc.","Skåne University Hospital","Turku University Hospital","University of Gothenburg","University of Helsinki","University of Pennsylvania","University of Turku"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Antibodies Monoclonal adverse effects","Cohort Studies","Colitis Ulcerative drug therapy","Denmark epidemiology","Female","Humans","Incidence","Longitudinal Studies","Male","Middle Aged","Sweden epidemiology","Tumor Necrosis Factor Inhibitors adverse effects"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-SDR"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-SDR","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a longitudinal post authorization safety study of golimumab in treatment of ulcerative colitis a cohort study in denmark and sweden 000000002013 000000002021","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40264-025-01519-8","ve