es Center Sjælland","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Roskilde University","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-88036592-20f9-4508-a101-431aecba3d83","ruc-7ec033e2-ea40-4b38-b04e-60ee0b0a80c8"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Børn af forældre med type 2-diabetes : Hvem er de, og hvordan er deres levevilkår, trivsel og sundhedsadfærd?","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-089.47-804510","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: The association between long-term exposure to air pollution and mortality from cardiorespiratory diseases is well established, yet the evidence for other diseases remains limited. OBJECTIVES: To examine the associations of long-term exposure to air pollution with mortality from diabetes, dementia, psychiatric disorders, chronic kidney disease (CKD), asthma, acute lower respiratory infection (ALRI), as well as mortality from all-natural and cardiorespiratory causes in the Danish nationwide administrative cohort. METHODS: We followed all residents aged ≥ 30 years (3,083,227) in Denmark from 1 January 2000 until 31 December 2017. Annual mean concentrations of fine particulate matter (PM 2.5), nitrogen dioxide (NO 2), black carbon (BC), and ozone (warm season) were estimated using European-wide hybrid land-use regression models (100 m × 100 m) and assigned to baseline residential addresses. We used Cox proportional hazard models to evaluate the association between air pollution and mortality, accounting for demographic and socioeconomic factors. We additionally applied indirect adjustment for smoking and body mass index (BMI). RESULTS: During 47,023,454 person-years of follow-up, 803,881 people died from natural causes. Long-term exposure to PM 2.5 (mean: 12.4 µg/m 3), NO 2 (20.3 µg/m 3), and/or BC (1.0 × 10 -5/m) was statistically significantly associated with all studied mortality outcomes except CKD. A 5 µg/m 3 increase in PM 2.5 was associated with higher mortality from all-natural causes (hazard ratio 1.11; 95% confidence interval 1.09-1.13), cardiovascular disease (1.09; 1.07-1.12), respiratory disease (1.11; 1.07-1.15), lung cancer (1.19; 1.15-1.24), diabetes (1.10; 1.04-1.16), dementia (1.05; 1.00-1.10), psychiatric disorders (1.38; 1.27-1.50), asthma (1.13; 0.94-1.36), and ALRI (1.14; 1.09-1.20). Associations with long-term exposure to ozone (mean: 80.2 µg/m 3) were generally negative but became significantly positive for several endpoints in two-pollutant models. Generally, associations were attenuated but remained significant after indirect adjustment for smoking and BMI. CONCLUSION: Long-term exposure to PM 2.5, NO 2, and/or BC in Denmark were associated with mortality beyond cardiorespiratory diseases, including diabetes, dementia, psychiatric disorders, asthma, and ALRI.","author_count":33,"contributors":["So, Rina","Andersen, Zorana Jovanovic","Chen, Jie","Stafoggia, Massimo","de Hoogh, Kees","Katsouyanni, Klea","Vienneau, Danielle","Rodopoulou, Sophia","Samoli, Evangelia","Lim, Youn-Hee","Jørgensen, Jeanette T","Amini, Heresh","Cole-Hunter, Thomas","Mahmood Taghavi Shahri, Seyed","Maric, Matija","Bergmann, Marie","Liu, Shuo","Azam, Shadi","Loft, Steffen","Westendorp, Rudi G J","Mortensen, Laust Hvas","Bauwelinck, Mariska","Klompmaker, Jochem O","Atkinson, Richard","Janssen, Nicole A H","Oftedal, Bente","Renzi, Matteo","Forastiere, Francesco","Strak, Maciek","Thygesen, Lau C","Brunekreef, Bert","Hoek, Gerard","Mehta, Amar J"],"cuid":["S02-099.27-045786"],"filters":{"added":"2022-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["So, Rina"],"country":["Belgium","Germany","Greece","Italy","Netherlands","Norway","Sweden","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Centre for Infectious Disease Control","Free University of Brussels","Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health","Karolinska Institute","Karolinska Institutet","King's College London","Lazio Regional Health Service","National Institute for Public Health and the Environment","National and Kapodistrian University of Athens","Norwegian Institute of Public Health","St. George's University of London","Swiss Tropical and Public Health Institute (Swiss TPH)","University of Basel","University of Regensburg","Utrecht University"],"keyword":["Dementia","Mortality","Psychiatric disorders"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Interface Demography - Department of Sociology"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"long term exposure to air pollution and mortality in a danish nationwide administrative cohort study beyond mortality from cardiopulmonary disease and lung cancer","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2022.107241","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work was supported by funding from HEI Research Agreement (#4954-RFA14-3/16-5-3), jointly funded by the United States Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) (Assistance Award No. R-82811201) and certain motor vehicle and engine manufacturers, and by the Novo Nordisk Foundation Challenge Programme: Harnessing the Power of Big Data to Address the Societal Challenge of Aging (#NNF17OC0027812). The funders had no role in the design of the study; the collection, analysis, and interpretation of the data; the writing of the manuscript; and the decision to submit the manuscript for publication The research described in this article was conducted under contract with the Health Effects Institute (HEI), an organization jointly funded by the United States Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) (Assistance Award No. R-82811201 ) and certain motor vehicle and engine manufacturers. The contents of this article do not necessarily reflect the views of HEI or its sponsors, nor do they necessarily reflect the views and policies of the EPA or motor vehicle and engine manufacturers."],"id":"S02-099.27-045786","identifier":[{"id":"85129776362","type":"scopus"},{"id":"35544998","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-bd4f18e1-672e-48f9-a9be-c1110d1544b1"